<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Óg de Burgh, 2nd Earl of Ulster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1259–<a href="/years/1326">1326</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Red Earl of Ulster · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">House of Burgh</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard de Burgh, known as the Red Earl, was earl of Ulster and lord of Connacht, the most powerful Anglo-Norman lord in Ireland around 1300. He was overlord of the O'Neills, built castles such as Ballymote and Northburgh and fought for Edward I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. His daughter Elizabeth married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>, whose brother Edward invaded Ireland in 1315.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Richard was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-de-burgh-1st-earl-of-ulster">Walter de Burgh, 1st Earl of Ulster</a> and Lord of Connacht, and Aveline FitzJohn, daughter of Sir John FitzGeoffrey. His grandfather was Richard Mór de Burgh, 1st Lord of Connaught. The name Richard Óg, Richard the Young, may refer to his youth when his father died or may distinguish him from that grandfather. He took up his inheritance in Ulster and Connacht on reaching his majority in 1280, and married Margaret, daughter of his cousin John de Burgh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Richard fought in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the 1280s and campaigned hard in Ulster. A close friend of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>, he was summoned again and again to the Scottish wars, stood at the head of the earls of Ireland and was made general of the forces in Ireland. In 1303 he sailed from Ireland with a large fleet to join Edward's army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. His expansion brought him into conflict with the FitzGeralds: in 1294 John FitzGerald, 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-kildare">Earl of Kildare</a>, captured him and held him at Lea Castle for several months. After his release <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> had to surrender his lands in Connacht to Richard, and Richard's daughter Joan married Kildare's heir.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-bruce">Edward Bruce</a> invaded Ulster in 1315, Richard led a force against him and was defeated at Connor. In 1316 Robert de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a>, Mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, suspecting him of having brought Bruce to Ireland, attacked St. Mary's Abbey, where he was staying, and seized him. With the rising of Felim McHugh O'Connor in Connacht he lost almost all authority in his lands, but O'Connor was killed at the Second Battle of Athenry in 1316, and Richard recovered Ulster after Bruce's defeat at Faughart in 1318.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Richard died on 29 July 1326 at Athassel Priory, near Cashel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>. Under him the earldom of Ulster was wealthy; around 150 people lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrickfergus">Carrickfergus</a> castle when he was in residence. His children married into the leading families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland, and the Earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a>, Desmond and Louth were among his sons-in-law. His daughter Elizabeth, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>, was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ii-of-scotland">David II of Scotland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard Óg de Burgh, 2nd Earl of Ulster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Richard de Burgh</b>, the Red Earl, was the most powerful lord in Ireland around 1300, overlord of the O'Neills and lord of much of Connacht.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-ulster">Earldom of Ulster</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Athassel Priory, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.48,-7.98&amp;year=1302" aria-label="Athassel Priory on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4371 353 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Richard Óg de Burgh, 2nd Earl of Ulster"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4821 623h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4821 623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.48,-7.98&amp;year=1302">What was here in 1302? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
